{
  "example": "A definition named `glossary.first_parent_history` declares the phrase \"first-parent history\". A term reference to that name renders the phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to the declaration, without displaying the identifier or a badge. {cite}`requirements.definitions.references`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the example is covered; one assertion is weaker than it could be.**\n\n**Scenario.** A definition declares a phrase. A `{term}` reference to it renders the phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to the anchored declaration. The identifier is not shown and there is no badge.\n\n**Test.** `tests/definitions.rs`, `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved` (lines 229–264), carries `{test}requirements.definitions.phrase_rendering`. It is the only `{test}` reference to this example.\n- **Setup.** `glossary.md` declares `{definition} a` with `:phrase: *alpha* & <beta>`. `guide/deep.md` contains a `{term}` reference to `glossary.a`. The setup also contains a reference in an inline-code example, one in a fenced block and one escaped. These are other behaviours; they do not interfere with the example.\n- **Declaration.** The test asserts the declaration is anchored (`id=\"habeas-definition-glossary.a\"`) and that the `:phrase:` option line is not rendered.\n- **No badge.** It asserts the referencing chapter has no `shields.io`.\n- **Link.** It asserts exactly one `href=\"../glossary.html#habeas-definition-glossary.a\"`. That is a correct relative link from `guide/deep.md` to the declaration anchor.\n- **Phrase.** It asserts the link text contains the phrase, correctly escaped: `&#42;alpha&#42; &amp; &lt;beta&gt;`.\n\n**Implementation, read as context.**\n- `replace_references` in `src/lib.rs` emits `<a href=\"…\">{escaped_phrase}</a>` for a `Term` reference. It uses no badge and no class, and the link text is only the phrase.\n- `replace_definitions` emits the declaration as `<strong id=\"habeas-definition-…\">phrase</strong>` followed by the body.\n- `terms::escaped_phrase` escapes Markdown and HTML specials.\n- So the rendered output matches the example. The example's phrase \"first-parent history\" is just a simpler instance; the test uses a harder, escaping-heavy phrase.\n\n**Residual weakness.** The test never asserts directly that the identifier is absent from the link text, nor that the link is exactly `<a …>phrase</a>`. The implementation shows only the phrase, and the test would catch a badge. The test does not assert the exact link markup, so the identifier-hidden outcome is only implicitly covered. I did not treat this as a gap, because the scenario, the plain link, the anchored target and the phrase text are all asserted.",
